  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1201 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nBroad high pressure will continue over the western CONUS and will be \nexpansive enough to cover the Southern High Plains as well as the \nsoutheast CONUS.  As a result we'll remain warm and dry with highs \nin the 80s Wednesday and through the weekend.  Previous forecasts \nhad noted that we might have an upper level trough move in over the \nweekend, but it looks to be delayed, and may not move in until early \nto mid next week.  Now there is some interesting activity to the \nsouth with the residual moisture from the tropical systems in both \nthe eastern Pacific and the Gulf.  At any given time we could get a \nlow form, off the Eastern Pacific or the Gulf and possibly become \ncutoff and drift up to the Panhandles.  Now, there's still a very \nlow probability of that (<15%), but at this time that would seem to \nbe the only reason pops would need to be added into the extended, as \nit's currently a dry forecast through Sunday. \n\nWeber",
